# Architecture Coherence LLM Guide

## Purpose

Use this command to review deterministic coherence signals for feature-module streams in the configured abstract tree: naming, tagged tree shape, sibling patterns, layer flow, public seam use, and dependency pressure.

## When To Run

- Before planning a module streamlining effort.
- When comparing several modules that should follow the same local shape.
- After introducing or reshaping a feature module.

## How To Read The Result

- Each stream has separate axis scores; low scores point at the area to inspect.
- Sibling-pattern findings identify streams whose tree shape differs from nearby streams under the same parent.
- Findings include the stream, axis, subject, detail, and concrete path or import evidence.
- Dependency pressure shows coupling signals; it is not an ownership rule by itself.
- This command is advisory and should not replace boundary or shape validation.

## Examples

- If tree-shape is low, inspect missing API seams, untagged tree nodes, or mixed root/nested layer shapes.
- If sibling-pattern is low, compare the stream to siblings before deciding whether it is special or merely drift.
- If flow is low, inspect internal imports between layers or unlayered files.
- If seam use is low, replace cross-stream internal imports with public seam imports when that fits the contract.
- If naming is low, look for path-segment style drift before standardizing a recipe.

## Agent Tips

- Use `--top -1` for a complete refactoring survey.
- Prefer fixing boundary and shape failures before using coherence for polish.
- Do not suggest automatic moves from this report alone; verify ownership and public seams first.
